  <header>
    <span class="info-eyebrow">Colophon</span>
    <h1 class="info-title">How this archive is made</h1>
    <p class="info-lead">
      A provenance note for readers, and an honest account of the machinery: what
      this site is, who keeps it, how it is built, and what it deliberately does
      not do.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="info-section" aria-labelledby="what-heading">
    <h2 id="what-heading">What this site is</h2>
    <p>
      A free archive of the Khyapa Parampara of Tarapith: the teachings, sadhana
      and sacred texts carried by Param Guru Bama Khyapa, Gurubaba Shyama Khyapa
      and Shri Praveen Radhakrishnan, together with the devotional art that
      accompanies them. Everything is open to read. There is no account to create,
      no paywall, and no advertising.
    </p>
    <ul class="info-facts">
      <li><b>Articles</b><span>{{ collections.articles | size | commafy }} written articles, browsable by topic, keyword and A&ndash;Z.</span></li>
      <li><b>Sacred art</b><span>{{ galleryImages | size }} works in the <a href="/gallery/">gallery</a>, each viewable at its native resolution.</span></li>
      <li><b>Books</b><span>Sahasranama and 108-names editions in English and Hindi, free to <a href="/ebooks/">download</a>.</span></li>
      <li><b>Audio</b><span>A continuous stream of recorded talks on <a href="/radio/">Guru Vani Radio</a>.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="info-section" aria-labelledby="who-heading">
    <h2 id="who-heading">Who keeps it</h2>
    <p>
      The archive is compiled and maintained by <strong>KaliPutra-Ashish</strong>,
      a disciple in this lineage, and offered in service to the Guru Parampara — it
      is a work of devotion, not a publication house. The
      <a href="/about/">About page</a> introduces the three teachers whose words
      and forms fill it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The code behind the site is public:
      <a href="https://github.com/09ashishkapoor/jaikhyapaparampara" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/09ashishkapoor/jaikhyapaparampara</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="info-section" aria-labelledby="built-heading">
    <h2 id="built-heading">How it is built</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A static site.</strong> Every page is pre-rendered HTML generated
        by <a href="https://www.11ty.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Eleventy</a>
        and served from Cloudflare Pages. There is no database and no server-side
        application to log into.</li>
      <li><strong>Almost no JavaScript.</strong> A few small vanilla scripts power
        on-site search, the gallery lightbox, the mobile menu and the radio player.
        Everything else is plain HTML and CSS.</li>
      <li><strong>One shared stylesheet.</strong> Every page loads the same style
        sheet, so moving between articles costs nothing extra. The temple palette
        of deep maroon, parchment and antique gold is the intended reading
        environment — there is no light mode, and none is planned.</li>
      <li><strong>Self-hosted fonts.</strong> Crimson Text and Philosopher are
        served from this domain as woff2 subsets under the SIL Open Font License.
        No request leaves the site to fetch a typeface.</li>
      <li><strong>One social card per article.</strong> Each article has a
        generated 1200&times;630 card at
        <code>/images/og/&lt;slug&gt;.jpg</code>, so a shared link shows the
        article's own title instead of a cropped image.</li>
      <li><strong>Feeds.</strong> The newest articles are published at
        <a href="/feed.xml">/feed.xml</a>, with a separate feed for each of the
        {{ collections.browseGroups | size }} <a href="/topics/">topic paths</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="info-section" aria-labelledby="measure-heading">
    <h2 id="measure-heading">What is measured, and what is not</h2>
    <p>
      The site carries <strong>no client-side analytics and no advertising
      tracker</strong>. There are no cookies to consent to and no profile built
      around a reader. Where the site measures anything, it uses two aggregate
      sources:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cloudflare Web Analytics</strong> — cookieless page-load and
        performance counts collected at the edge, without a per-reader identifier.</li>
      <li><strong>Google Search Console</strong> — which queries and pages Google
        showed, so the archive can be made easier to find.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Neither can reconstruct a reading session, and neither is used to profile
      anyone. If a future feature would need per-reader tracking, it will not be
      added here.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="info-section" aria-labelledby="art-heading">
    <h2 id="art-heading">The sacred-art archive</h2>
    <p>
      The gallery masters are the archive. They are kept exactly as received, at
      their native resolution — up to 5600&nbsp;px — and are never resized,
      re-compressed or overwritten. Smaller WebP derivatives exist only so that
      the grid and the lightbox can load quickly; the
      <em>View original</em> link in the lightbox always opens the untouched
      master, and the gallery's structured data points at the masters too.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="info-section" aria-labelledby="words-heading">
    <h2 id="words-heading">Words, transcripts and translations</h2>
    <p>
      Mantras, quotations and recorded talks are published as received. They are
      not reworded for style, and transcript text is not edited to read more
      smoothly. Where a reader needs context — who is speaking, what a term means,
      where a passage comes from — an attributed note is placed <em>before</em> the
      transcript and set apart from it, so the sacred text and the editorial note
      are never confused for one another.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="info-section" aria-labelledby="share-heading">
    <h2 id="share-heading">Sharing, and corrections</h2>
    <p>
      All knowledge here is forever free to share: quote it, print it, pass it on.
      The site code is open source; the typefaces are under the SIL Open Font
      License.
    </p>
    <p>
      If something is wrong — a mistyped mantra, a broken link, a misspelled name
      — please say so through the repository's issue templates. Corrections to
      transcript or sacred text are handled with particular care, and never
      silently.
    </p>
    <p class="info-note">
      This page describes the machinery, not the teaching. For the lineage itself,
      read <a href="/about/">About the Parampara</a>; for what has changed here
      over time, see the <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
